#!/bin/sh
# pr-ci: the yard as the CI of a pull request. It runs the depot's test
# command on the head of the pull request a pr bead tracks, merged into the
# base, when the bead is in ciwait, and says how it went: done, with the head it
# tested recorded as the bead's metadata ci_head, which merge-pr reads before it
# merges anything; or changes, with the failing tests and the end of the output,
# and the full output attached beside it, or with the conflicts of a head that
# does not merge into the base. Run it as the cmd of an exec group of its
# own (pr-ci, limit 1: one run at a time), routed for the pr flow's ciwait stage
# (type pr). The contributors pack's flows/pr/ciwait.md says what the stage is
# for, and docs/line.md, "The yard as the CI of a pull request", has the setup.
#
# The pull request is the one the bead tracks (its tracks link, whose URL must be
# https://github.com/<owner>/<repo>/pull/<n> and nothing else), never one found
# by a name in metadata or by a branch. GitHub is asked as an installation token
# of the yard's App scoped to that repository, as merge-pr asks it, minted by
# contrib/github-mint (the github source's mint_command, or YARDR_GITHUB_MINT in
# the group's cmd) from GITHUB_APP_ID and GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE, which the
# group's env_file supplies; the operator's own gh login is never used. Needs gh
# and jq on PATH. Nothing is written to GitHub: no commit status, no check run.
# The token is narrowed to pull_requests read alone (PERMS below): reading a
# pull request wants Pull requests read of it (GitHub's REST docs, "Get a
# pull request"), and the calls ask for the number, state and head alone, no
# check or status field.
#
# What is tested is the head the reviewer read, merged into the base. The
# reviewer records the commit it read as the bead's metadata reviewed_head
# before it reports done (the contributors pack's roles/pr-reviewer.md), and the
# head GitHub says the pull request has now is tested only if it is that
# commit: a pull request pushed to between the review and this run, whose event
# came late or not at all, holds the bead for a new review before anything of it
# is fetched or run. A bead with no reviewed_head holds too, since nothing says
# which head was read.
#
# And, for a pull request that waited in triage, the head a maintainer accepted.
# The bead carries that commit as its metadata accepted_head (the yard records
# the head the accepting event named, and a maintainer who moved the bead by
# hand records the one they read), and the head is tested only if it is that
# commit: a head no person judged is not run, however the bead came back to
# ciwait. A bead that never waited in triage, one the pack's own lines let into
# review (a trusted author's, a bot's), is held to the reviewer's record alone.
#
# The code is the head GitHub says the pull request has now, merged into the
# base. The head is fetched from the depot's remote origin by the ref GitHub
# keeps for the pull request, a fork's included (git fetch origin
# refs/pull/<n>/head), and so is the base: the branch of origin that the
# depot's base names (main, for main and origin/main alike), as origin has it
# now and not as the depot last saw it. Both are fetched with the operator's own
# git credentials, and neither moves a ref of the depot. git merge-tree merges
# the head into the base in the object store alone; a clean merge is made a
# commit of two parents, the base and the head, that no ref names, and that
# commit is checked out detached in a worktree of its own beside the bead's,
# which is removed when the script ends, however it ends. A bead that is not in
# ciwait is refused before anything is fetched: ciwait is the stage after a
# reviewer has read the pull request, and the yard runs a pull request's code
# nowhere before that.
#
# The merge is what is tested because it is the nearest a test gets to what
# landing makes: merge-pr has GitHub merge the head into the base. A head that
# is only behind the base, tested alone, would be judged by the base's rules on
# a tree the base has since left (a check it tightened, a test it mended), a
# failure that only merging the base cures and that GitHub never asks of a pull
# request. The head stays what is reviewed, accepted, recorded (ci_head) and
# merged; only the tree that is tested is the merge. The base is the commit
# origin had when the run began, which the notes name, and it is not asked again
# before the pull request is merged. A head that conflicts with the base has no
# merge to test: it is changes, with the conflicts and the cure, merging the
# base into the pull request's branch, which the contributor does.
#
# The contributors pack carries a copy of this script (scripts/pr-ci) for the
# group that works ciwait, which a test holds equal: a change is made to both.
#
# It reads the environment the exec runner gives it: YARDR_BEAD, YARDR_DEPOT,
# YARDR_DEPOT_PATH, YARDR_BASE, YARDR_WORKTREE, YARDR_BIN and YARDR_TEST, the
# depot's test command (yardr depot add --test), unset for a depot with none.
# Option, set in the group's cmd:
#
#   YARDR_TEST_TIMEOUT  seconds the test command may run, besides what it
#                       waited for the race lock (default 1800)
#
# The test command is the one every landing runs, run as merge-local runs it,
# in the checkout of the merge and with YARDR_LANDING=1. This run is the pull
# request's landing run: merge-pr merges a head it passed on and no landing by
# the assembly follows, so no other run of the command stands between the head
# and the base. Where the command is the path of a file the base has
# (test=.yardr/test), the base's copy of that file is what runs, with sh, the
# one of the base commit that was merged, so that the script and the tree agree
# on the base's side: a pull request cannot change the script that judges it,
# which matters most for a fork's. Anything else is a command for sh -c, which
# the pull request's own files may change (a package.json's scripts); the note
# says which ran. The run does not get the App's id and key file, which are for
# the token alone.
#
# Exit 1, or any other not named here, is a red run, the contributor's to fix.
# Exit 2 is the command saying it could not run at all (a tool missing, a base
# it cannot find; closefds says it too, for a descriptor above 9 that this sh
# cannot close), which no contributor can fix: held. An exit above 128 means
# the run was ended by a signal from outside, which says nothing about the pull
# request: it is run once more, and held if a signal ends it again. The kill at
# the deadline is not this: it is a red run.
#
# The outcomes at ciwait are done and changes. Green on the merge of the head,
# which GitHub still says is the head after the run, is done; a red run, a head
# that does not merge into the base, or a head that moved while it ran (the
# tests said nothing of the head it has now), is changes. Done is never reported
# without a green run on a merge of the exact head. Everything that is no
# verdict on the pull request (no token, a refused gh, a failed fetch of the
# head or of the base, a base branch origin lacks, a merge git cannot make, a
# depot with no test command, a run that could not run, a pull request that is
# not open, a head no reviewer read) holds the bead with a note, for a
# maintainer to put right and unhold. Nothing here retries.
#
# Every outcome ends in a report or a hold: exiting without one counts as a
# failed session, and three of those hold the bead.
set -u
# A dir depot (YARDR_DEPOT_KIND=dir) has no repository to fetch a pull request
# into. A route that hands one over is a mistake in the depot's flow, so refuse
# to run (exit 2) rather than guess.
if [ "${YARDR_DEPOT_KIND:-git}" = dir ]; then
  echo "pr-ci: depot ${YARDR_DEPOT:-?} is a dir depot, with no repository to fetch a pull request into; route its stages to other groups" >&2
  exit 2
fi
: "${YARDR_BEAD:?not run by an exec group}" "${YARDR_DEPOT:?bead has no depot}"
: "${YARDR_DEPOT_PATH:?}" "${YARDR_BASE:?}"
Y=${YARDR_BIN:-yardr}
BEAD=$YARDR_BEAD
DEPOT=$YARDR_DEPOT
REPO=$YARDR_DEPOT_PATH
BASE=$YARDR_BASE
TEST=${YARDR_TEST:-}
LIMIT=${YARDR_TEST_TIMEOUT:-1800}
TAIL=200
# How many failing tests a red note keeps from the full log, ahead of the
# tail: a failure early in a long run would otherwise sit unseen above it.
FAILS=40
# The head's own checkout, beside the bead's worktree and never in it. The path
# is the same every run of the bead, so what a killed run left is found and
# cleared by the next (scratch_gone), and it is no bead's id, so no bead has it.
WT=${YARDR_WORKTREE:-${YARDR_HOME:-$HOME/.yardr}/worktrees/$DEPOT/$BEAD}-ci
echo "== $(date '+%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S%z') pr-ci $DEPOT $BEAD"

# tree prints a process and everything below it, in one order every time.
tree() {
  ps -A -o pid= -o ppid= | awk -v root="$1" '
    { parent[$1] = $2 }
    END {
      below[root] = 1
      do { more = 0; for (p in parent) if (!(p in below) && (parent[p] in below)) { below[p] = 1; more = 1 } } while (more)
      for (p in below) print p
    }' | sort -n
}
# unwatched kills the timeout watcher and its sleep, after a run and on
# every exit while one is in flight (the traps): a watcher left behind
# sleeps out its $LIMIT holding the run's inherited files, among them once
# the machine race lock (yardr-tsms). The sleep goes by the pid the watcher
# wrote, in case the watcher died without killing it, and both kills are
# silent: either may already be gone. What the file holds is signalled only
# if it is a number with a non-zero digit: kill 0 signals this script's whole
# process group, and kill reads 00 as 0 (yardr-0bd1).
unwatched() {
  [ -n "${watch:-}" ] || return 0
  kill "$watch" 2>/dev/null
  if [ -n "${napfile:-}" ] && [ -s "$napfile" ]; then
    nap=$(cat "$napfile" 2>/dev/null) || nap=
    case "$nap" in ''|*[!0-9]*) ;; *[1-9]*) kill "$nap" 2>/dev/null ;; esac
  fi
  wait "$watch" 2>/dev/null
  watch=
}
# rundone removes the files of a run, whichever of them exist: when the run is
# over, and by tidy.
rundone() {
  unwatched
  [ -z "${log:-}" ] || rm -f "$log"
  [ -z "${latefile:-}" ] || rm -f "$latefile"
  [ -z "${napfile:-}" ] || rm -f "$napfile"
  [ -z "${copy:-}" ] || rm -f "$copy"
  return 0
}
# scratch_gone removes the head's checkout and its registration in the depot's
# repository, in whatever state a run left them: a worktree whose directory is
# gone is only a record, one with files in it is removed with them, and a
# directory git never registered is removed as it is.
scratch_gone() {
  [ -n "${WT:-}" ] || return 0
  git -C "$REPO" worktree remove --force "$WT" >/dev/null 2>&1
  rm -rf "${WT:?}"
  git -C "$REPO" worktree prune >/dev/null 2>&1
  return 0
}
# stoprun stops the command the script started, and every process below it,
# before its checkout is removed. A session can be stopped while its tests are
# still running; removing the worktree alone would leave those tests running
# against files that no longer exist.
stoprun() {
  [ -n "${pid:-}" ] || return 0
  seen=
  while t=$(tree "$pid") && [ "$t" != "$seen" ]; do
    # shellcheck disable=SC2086
    kill -STOP $t 2>/dev/null
    seen=$t
  done
  # shellcheck disable=SC2086
  kill -KILL ${seen:-$pid} 2>/dev/null
  wait "$pid" 2>/dev/null
  pid=
}
# tidy stops a run and removes the checkout of the pull request's code.
tidy() {
  unwatched
  stoprun
  rundone
  scratch_gone
}
# bye takes away everything the script made, whichever of it exists. It is the
# first thing hold and report do, so that nothing is left to do once the session
# ends (the daemon stops the script as soon as it has reported), and the EXIT
# trap, for every other way out. The directory the script works in goes last,
# and from outside it. TERM and INT are ignored while it runs, so that a stop of
# the session that comes meanwhile cannot leave it half done.
bye() {
  trap '' TERM INT
  tidy
  cd /
  [ -z "${work:-}" ] || rm -rf "${work:?}"
  return 0
}
# pid is only the test command this script starts, never a handle inherited
# from the exec group's environment.
unset pid
pid=
trap 'bye' EXIT
# A signal that kills the script does not run its EXIT trap, so TERM and
# INT clean up first and then report as a shell would, 128+n.
trap 'unwatched; exit 143' TERM
trap 'unwatched; exit 130' INT

# Reporting ends this session (the daemon stops the script), so it is
# always the last thing done.
hold() { bye; echo "== hold $BEAD: $1"; "$Y" bead note "$BEAD" "pr-ci: $1"; "$Y" bead hold "$BEAD"; exit 0; }
report() {
  bye
  echo "== $BEAD $1"
  printf '%s\n' "pr-ci: $2" | "$Y" bead "done" "$BEAD" --outcome "$1" --note - && exit 0
  # The pull request's own event may have moved the bead first: a push says
  # changes to a bead in ciwait, or pushed (back to review) when a bot made
  # it, and a head that moved is what this report would have said. A bead that
  # left ciwait is where the report would have taken it.
  left=$("$Y" bead show "$BEAD" --json 2>/dev/null | jq -r '.stage // empty')
  [ -z "$left" ] || [ "$left" = ciwait ] || { echo "== $BEAD is in $left already"; exit 0; }
  hold "could not report $1 (does the bead's flow name it for its stage?): $2"
}
# gh is run from a directory that is no checkout, so nothing it does to a
# local branch can reach anyone's work; the run's files are in it too.
work=$(mktemp -d) && errf=$work/err || exit 1
cd "$work" || exit 1

command -v gh >/dev/null 2>&1 || hold "gh is not on PATH; not running the tests."
command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1 || hold "jq is not on PATH; not running the tests."

# With --json yardr reports an error as {"error": ...} on stdout. Capture
# stderr for the hold note, then replay it so warnings stay in the group's log.
bead=$("$Y" bead show "$BEAD" --json 2>"$errf") || {
  reason=$({ cat "$errf"; printf '%s' "$bead"; } | head -c 500)
  cat "$errf" >&2
  hold "reading $BEAD failed: $reason"
}
cat "$errf" >&2

# The code of a pull request is run only after a reviewer has read it, which
# is what getting to ciwait means. A route that sends another stage's bead here
# (a typo, a copy of the pack's flow under other names) must not run unread code.
[ "$(printf '%s' "$bead" | jq -r '.stage // empty')" = ciwait ] ||
  hold "$BEAD is not in ciwait, the stage after a reviewer has read the pull request, and the yard runs a pull request's code nowhere before it. Not running anything."

# A pull request is not called green untested: with no command there is
# nothing to run as its CI, and that is the depot's to put right.
[ -n "$TEST" ] ||
  hold "depot $DEPOT has no test command, so the yard has nothing to run as the CI of the pull request, and a pull request is not called green untested. Set one (yardr depot add $DEPOT $REPO --test <command>), then unhold. Not running anything."

NOT="not running the tests" STAGE=ciwait
# The token's permissions, the least the gh calls below need: reading a pull
# request wants Pull requests read of the token (GitHub's REST docs, "Get a
# pull request"), and the calls ask for the number, state and head alone, no
# check or status field. The shared lines pass them to the mint, which posts
# them in the access_tokens body.
PERMS='{"pull_requests":"read"}'
# --- github_binding: the same text in merge-pr, pr-ci and push-fix (a test holds them equal) ---
# NOT, STAGE and PERMS are each script's own: what it leaves undone and where
# the bead waits, and the least permissions its token needs; everything else
# is one text.
#
# The pull request is the one the bead tracks, by the URL of the link and
# nothing else: owner, repository and number are read from it, and a URL of
# another shape (another host, an issue, a path past the number) is refused.
url=$(printf '%s' "$bead" | jq -r '[.links[]? | select(.kind == "tracks" and .source == "github") | .url] | first // empty')
[ -n "$url" ] ||
  hold "$BEAD tracks no GitHub pull request (no tracks link of source github); $NOT. Link it with yardr link, or take the bead out of $STAGE."
printf '%s' "$url" | grep -Eq '^https://github\.com/[A-Za-z0-9]([A-Za-z0-9-]*[A-Za-z0-9])?/[A-Za-z0-9._-]+/pull/[1-9][0-9]*$' ||
  hold "$BEAD tracks $url, which is not a pull request address (https://github.com/<owner>/<repo>/pull/<n>); $NOT."
path=${url#https://github.com/}
owner=${path%%/*}
rest=${path#*/}
repo=${rest%%/*}
case $repo in . | ..) hold "$BEAD tracks $url, which is not a pull request address (https://github.com/<owner>/<repo>/pull/<n>); $NOT." ;; esac
slug=$owner/$repo

# The token: minted for this repository alone, from the App's id and key file
# the group's env_file gives, and held in this process's memory for the calls
# below. It is passed to gh in its environment, never in an argument, and is
# printed nowhere. The mint command is the github source's own unless the
# group's cmd names another. The token is narrowed to the permissions PERMS
# names, each script's own least set above, which the mint posts in the
# access_tokens body; a name or level outside the mint's list refuses the mint.
[ -n "${GITHUB_APP_ID:-}" ] && [ -n "${GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE:-}" ] ||
  hold "this group's environment has no GITHUB_APP_ID and GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE, which the yard's GitHub App is minted from (the group's env_file); $NOT."
mint=${YARDR_GITHUB_MINT:-}
[ -n "$mint" ] || mint=$("$Y" source show github --json 2>/dev/null | jq -r '.source.mint_command // empty')
[ -n "$mint" ] ||
  hold "the github source has no mint_command and the group's cmd sets no YARDR_GITHUB_MINT; $NOT. Install contrib/github-mint, then yardr source set github mint_command=<its path>."
# mint_command has the same space-split, no-shell contract as yardr's source
# mint. Disable pathname expansion while forming argv so a configured `*`
# stays an argument and never grows into files in the runner's directory.
mint_token() {
  set -f
  # shellcheck disable=SC2086
  set -- $mint
  set +f
  [ "$#" -gt 0 ] || return 1
  YARDR_SOURCE_BASE=https://api.github.com "$@" 2>/dev/null
}
tokjson=$(jq -nc --arg id "$GITHUB_APP_ID" --arg key "$GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE" --arg scope "$slug" --argjson permissions "$PERMS" \
  '{inputs: {GITHUB_APP_ID: $id, GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE: $key}, scope: $scope, permissions: $permissions}' |
  mint_token) ||
  hold "minting a token for $slug failed; the mint's output is not copied to the bead. $NOT."
token=$(printf '%s' "$tokjson" | jq -r '.token // empty')
unset tokjson
[ -n "$token" ] || hold "minting a token for $slug answered no token; $NOT."
gh_() { GH_TOKEN=$token gh "$@"; }
# --- end of github_binding ---

short() { printf '%s' "$1" | cut -c1-7; }
lower() { printf '%s' "$1" | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]'; }
# live_head prints the head GitHub says the pull request has now, and nothing
# when it cannot say (the error is left in $errf).
live_head() { gh_ pr view "$url" --json number,state,headRefOid 2>"$errf" | jq -r '.headRefOid // empty'; }

pr=$(gh_ pr view "$url" --json number,state,headRefOid 2>"$errf") ||
  hold "reading pull request $url failed: $(head -c 500 "$errf")"
state=$(printf '%s' "$pr" | jq -r '.state // empty')
head=$(printf '%s' "$pr" | jq -r '.headRefOid // empty')

# A pull request that is not open has no head to answer for: it was merged or
# closed while the bead waited. The source says so (merged, dropped, which
# end the bead), but a run can find it over before the source has, and no
# event moves a held bead. A maintainer sees it on GitHub and moves the bead.
[ "$state" = OPEN ] ||
  hold "pull request $url is in state ${state:-unknown}, not OPEN, so there is no head to test. Not running anything."
[ -n "$head" ] || hold "GitHub gave no head commit for $url; not running the tests."
# The head goes into a path and into the arguments of git: a commit's sha and
# nothing else.
case $head in *[!0-9a-fA-F]*) head= ;; esac
case ${#head} in 40 | 64) ;; *) head= ;; esac
[ -n "$head" ] || hold "GitHub gave a head for $url that is no commit sha; not running the tests."
at=$(short "$head")
number=${url##*/}

# The head is the one a maintainer accepted, where a maintainer was asked, or
# nothing of it is fetched or run. A pull request that waited in triage was let
# in by a person looking at one commit: the bead carries it as accepted_head,
# which is read as a full sha and compared without regard to case, as the
# reviewer's record is. A head that differs, or none recorded, needs a person to
# say they accept the head as it now is; the record is theirs to write, so the
# command that does is named. Newer beads keep rounds, and older ones keep
# visited, so both say whether triage was reached.
triaged=$(printf '%s' "$bead" | jq -r 'if ((.rounds // {}) | has("triage")) or ((.visited // []) | index("triage") != null) then "yes" else empty end')
if [ -n "$triaged" ]; then
  accepted=$(printf '%s' "$bead" | jq -r '.metadata.accepted_head // empty')
  case $accepted in *[!0-9a-fA-F]*) accepted= ;; esac
  case ${#accepted} in 40 | 64) ;; *) accepted= ;; esac
  accept="A maintainer who has read the head records the commit they accept: yardr bead update $BEAD --meta accepted_head=<sha>, then yardr bead unhold $BEAD."
  [ -n "$accepted" ] ||
    hold "$BEAD waited in triage and has no accepted_head that is a full commit sha (40 hex digits; 64 for a repository on sha256), so there is no telling that the head of $url, $head, is the one a maintainer accepted. Not running anything. $accept"
  [ "$(lower "$accepted")" = "$(lower "$head")" ] ||
    hold "head $head is not the accepted $accepted: needs a maintainer's acceptance. Nothing of $url was fetched or run. $accept"
fi

# The head is the one a reviewer read, or nothing of it is fetched or run. The
# reviewer's record is taken as a full sha, as merge-pr takes merge_head, and
# compared without regard to case; anything else it holds is not echoed.
# Whatever differs, or is missing, needs a review of the head as it now is: the
# bead goes back to review (a person's command), and its reviewer records the
# head it then reads.
reviewed=$(printf '%s' "$bead" | jq -r '.metadata.reviewed_head // empty')
case $reviewed in *[!0-9a-fA-F]*) reviewed= ;; esac
case ${#reviewed} in 40 | 64) ;; *) reviewed= ;; esac
rereview="Send it back to be read: yardr bead advance $BEAD --to review, then yardr bead unhold $BEAD; its reviewer records the head it reads."
[ -n "$reviewed" ] ||
  hold "$BEAD has no reviewed_head that is a full commit sha (40 hex digits; 64 for a repository on sha256), so there is no telling that the head of $url, $head, was read. Not running anything. $rereview"
[ "$(lower "$reviewed")" = "$(lower "$head")" ] ||
  hold "head $head is not the reviewed $reviewed: needs re-review. Nothing of $url was fetched or run. $rereview"

# The head, fetched from the remote the pull request is on by the ref GitHub
# keeps for it (a fork's pull request has one too), with the operator's own git
# credentials and no prompt for any. What is wanted is the commit GitHub named
# above, whatever the ref holds by now; the sha is the whole of the claim, and
# a commit the fetch did not bring is not tested.
to=; command -v timeout >/dev/null && to="timeout 120"
# origin_git is git in the depot, asked of origin: with no prompt for a
# credential, and ended after two minutes where there is a timeout to end it.
origin_git() { GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0 GIT_ASKPASS=true SSH_ASKPASS=true $to git -C "$REPO" "$@"; }
origin_git fetch --quiet --no-tags origin "refs/pull/$number/head" 2>"$errf" ||
  hold "fetching refs/pull/$number/head from origin in $REPO failed: $(head -c 500 "$errf"). Not running anything."
if ! git -C "$REPO" cat-file -e "$head^{commit}" 2>/dev/null; then
  # Pushed to between GitHub's answer and the fetch, or origin is not the
  # repository the pull request is on. GitHub tells which.
  now=$(live_head)
  if [ -n "$now" ] && [ "$(lower "$now")" != "$(lower "$head")" ]; then
    report changes "the PR changed while CI started: GitHub said its head was $at and says $(short "$now") now. Nothing was run; the next commits bring it back to review."
  fi
  hold "origin did not give the commit $head of $url by refs/pull/$number/head: is origin the repository the pull request is on? Not running anything."
fi

# The base, as origin has it now and not as the depot last saw it, for the head
# is tested merged into it. The depot's base is a branch (main) or the
# remote-tracking one of origin (origin/main), and the branch is the same one of
# origin's either way. Its commit is asked for first and the branch fetched
# after, so that the commit is the one that was asked for: FETCH_HEAD, which
# any other fetch in the depot overwrites (merge-local's, say), is not trusted
# to say which it was. --refmap= leaves out the remote-tracking branch the fetch
# would move, so that no ref of the depot does.
bbranch=${BASE#origin/}
asked=$(origin_git ls-remote origin "refs/heads/$bbranch" 2>"$errf") ||
  hold "asking origin in $REPO for its branch $bbranch, which the depot's base $BASE names, failed: $(head -c 500 "$errf"). Not running anything."
basesha=$(printf '%s\n' "$asked" | awk -v ref="refs/heads/$bbranch" '$2 == ref { print $1; exit }')
case $basesha in *[!0-9a-fA-F]*) basesha= ;; esac
case ${#basesha} in 40 | 64) ;; *) basesha= ;; esac
[ -n "$basesha" ] ||
  hold "origin has no branch $bbranch, which the depot's base $BASE names, so there is nothing to merge the head of $url into. Not running anything."
origin_git fetch --quiet --no-tags --refmap= origin "refs/heads/$bbranch" 2>"$errf" ||
  hold "fetching $bbranch from origin in $REPO failed: $(head -c 500 "$errf"). Not running anything."
git -C "$REPO" cat-file -e "$basesha^{commit}" 2>/dev/null ||
  hold "origin did not give the commit $basesha of its branch $bbranch: was it pushed over while it was fetched? Not running anything; unhold runs the tests again."
bat=$(short "$basesha")
# What the notes name as what ran: the head, merged into the base.
tested="$at merged into $bbranch at $bat"

# The head merged into the base, by git merge-tree, which makes the merge in the
# object store alone: no checkout, no index, no ref of the depot is touched, and
# no hook runs. A clean merge answers its tree and exits 0. With conflicts it
# answers the tree it could make, the entries in conflict and what happened,
# and exits 1; but 1 is what git says as well of a commit it cannot merge at
# all, with nothing on stdout, which is no conflict of the pull request's: a
# conflict is a 1 that left a tree. Any other exit is git failing (128 for
# histories with nothing in common, 129 from a git older than 2.38, which has no
# --write-tree), and is no verdict on the pull request. The CONFLICT lines are
# picked out by their English prefix, so the locale is pinned.
mrc=0
mout=$(LC_ALL=C git -C "$REPO" merge-tree --write-tree "$basesha" "$head" 2>"$errf") || mrc=$?
tree=$(printf '%s\n' "$mout" | head -n 1)
case $tree in *[!0-9a-fA-F]*) tree= ;; esac
case ${#tree} in 40 | 64) ;; *) tree= ;; esac
if [ "$mrc" = 1 ] && [ -n "$tree" ]; then
  # The tree it could make is never checked out: it holds the conflict markers.
  conflicts=$(printf '%s\n' "$mout" | grep '^CONFLICT' | head -n 20)
  report changes "the head $at of $url does not merge into $bbranch at $bat, so there is nothing to test and nothing was run. To resolve the conflicts, merge $bbranch into the pull request's branch (or rebase it onto $bbranch) and push; the next commits bring it back to review. Git's CONFLICT lines, up to 20:
$conflicts"
fi
[ "$mrc" = 0 ] && [ -n "$tree" ] ||
  hold "git could not merge the head $at of $url into $bbranch at $bat (git merge-tree --write-tree, exit $mrc; a git older than 2.38 has none): $(head -c 500 "$errf"). Not running anything."
# The merge is made a commit so that the checkout is one: a detached HEAD the
# tests can name. commit-tree runs no hook and signs nothing, and wants an
# identity that no machine can be relied on to have; no ref names the commit,
# which the checkout's HEAD keeps until the checkout is removed.
merged=$(GIT_AUTHOR_NAME=pr-ci GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL=pr-ci@yardr.dev GIT_COMMITTER_NAME=pr-ci GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL=pr-ci@yardr.dev \
  git -C "$REPO" commit-tree "$tree" -p "$basesha" -p "$head" -m "pr-ci: $head of $url merged into $bbranch at $basesha" 2>"$errf") ||
  hold "cannot make the merge of $tested to test: $(head -c 500 "$errf")"
scratch_gone
git -C "$REPO" worktree add --detach --quiet "$WT" "$merged" >"$errf" 2>&1 ||
  hold "cannot check out the merge of $tested of $url in $WT: $(head -c 500 "$errf")"

# The depot's test command runs on the head GitHub named merged into the base,
# as merge-local runs it for a bead. Where the command is the path of a file the
# base has (test=.yardr/test), the base's copy of that file is what runs, with
# sh, in the checkout of the merge, the copy of the commit that was merged;
# anything else is a command for sh -c. With YARDR_LANDING=1: this is the pull
# request's landing run, and what a depot's command makes of that is its own.
if [ "$(git -C "$REPO" cat-file -t "$basesha:$TEST" 2>/dev/null)" = blob ]; then
  WHAT="the base's copy of $TEST"
else
  WHAT="the command $TEST"
fi
echo "== the tests on $url at $tested: $WHAT"

# --- closefds: the same text in merge-local, integrate-train, pr-ci, deploy-dokku and deploy-void (a test holds them equal) ---
# closefds closes every descriptor above 2 that the subshell it is called in
# inherited, so that what the subshell starts holds none the landing did not
# give it: the daemon's leaked resolver sockets once reached the tests and
# starved them (yardr-b4ep), and an orphaned sleep once held the machine race
# lock (yardr-tsms).
#
# 3 to 9 are closed by name, which every sh reads. Above them /dev/fd is
# enumerated, so that no number is missed; the loop has no redirection, since
# the shell saves fd 2 above 10 for one and would close the save from under
# itself. When the listing itself fails, closefds refuses what it never
# saw: a run that cannot prove its descriptors closed must not start.
# But dash reads one digit as a redirection's number: there
# `exec 10>&-` is the command `10` with its output closed, and a special
# builtin that fails ends the shell. A subshell asks first. An sh that cannot
# name them leaves them open, and closefds returns 1 with their numbers in
# $leftover; given the name of what the caller is about to start, it says so
# and exits 2 instead, the code for a run that could not be made rather than
# a failed one (the assembly scripts hold on it: no builder can fix it).
# Nothing hands this script such a descriptor since the daemon marks its own
# close-on-exec before it starts one, so one that arrives comes from a source
# that has regressed, which is why it is refused and not skipped.
closefds() {
  exec 3>&- 4>&- 5>&- 6>&- 7>&- 8>&- 9>&-
  leftover=
  highok=1
  (eval 'exec 10>&-') >/dev/null 2>&1 || highok=
  for path in /dev/fd/*; do
    if [ "$path" = "/dev/fd/*" ]; then
      # An unmatched glob expands to itself: the listing failed, so no
      # descriptor was seen, let alone closed. Refuse rather than
      # report them closed.
      leftover="$leftover ?"
      break
    fi
    fd=${path##*/}
    case "$fd" in ''|*[!0-9]*|?) continue ;; esac
    if [ -n "$highok" ]; then
      eval "exec $fd>&-"
    elif [ -e "$path" ] || [ -L "$path" ]; then
      leftover="$leftover $fd"
    fi
  done
  [ -z "$leftover" ] && return 0
  [ -n "${1:-}" ] || return 1
  echo "${0##*/}: descriptors$leftover are open above 9 and this sh cannot close them; not starting $1 with them" >&2
  exit 2
}
# --- end of closefds ---
# gradle_fails prints the failing tests a Gradle run left in the checkout:
# each testcase carrying a failure or an error in a TEST-*.xml file under
# $WT, named classname.name. Gradle's own output says only how many failed,
# so without this a red note names no test. One line per test, in the
# files' sorted order; nothing when there are no such files.
gradle_fails() {
  [ -n "${WT:-}" ] && [ -d "$WT" ] || return 0
  find "$WT" -name 'TEST-*.xml' -type f 2>/dev/null | LC_ALL=C sort | while IFS= read -r xf; do
    awk '
      function attr(line, key,   m) {
        if (match(line, "[ \t]" key "=\"[^\"]*\"")) {
          m = substr(line, RSTART + length(key) + 3, RLENGTH - length(key) - 4)
          return m
        }
        return ""
      }
      /<testcase[^>]*>/ {
        cls = attr($0, "classname"); nm = attr($0, "name")
        failed = ($0 ~ /<failure[ >\/]/ || $0 ~ /<error[ >\/]/) ? 1 : 0
        if ($0 ~ /<\/testcase>/) {
          if (failed) {
            if (cls != "" && nm != "") print cls "." nm
            else if (nm != "") print nm
          }
          cls = ""; nm = ""; failed = 0; incase = 0
        } else if ($0 ~ /\/>[ \t]*$/) {
          cls = ""; nm = ""; failed = 0; incase = 0
        } else {
          incase = 1
        }
        next
      }
      incase && (/<failure[ >\/]/ || /<error[ >\/]/) { failed = 1 }
      incase && /<\/testcase>/ {
        if (failed) {
          if (cls != "" && nm != "") print cls "." nm
          else if (nm != "") print nm
        }
        cls = ""; nm = ""; failed = 0; incase = 0
      }
    ' "$xf" 2>/dev/null
  done
}
# runtest runs the test command in the checkout and leaves its exit status in
# $rc, the last $TAIL lines of its output in $out, how many lines there were
# in $lines, the failing tests in $fails (the full log's FAIL and XCTest
# lines, and the Gradle names, deduped, at most $FAILS, only when the log
# runs past the tail), and $late set when it ran past $LIMIT seconds besides
# what it waited for the race lock. It adds the seconds the run took to $secs,
# which a second run after a signal adds to. A red run's full log stays in
# $log until the outcome attaches it to the bead; a green run's is removed
# at once, as ever. The bound is sh's own, as in merge-local, which says why
# it is made this way.
runtest() {
  # Each file of the run is made by a mktemp of its own, in the directory only
  # this script can enter.
  log=$(mktemp "$work/log.XXXXXX") || hold "cannot make a file for the tests' output"
  napfile=$(mktemp "$work/nap.XXXXXX") || hold "cannot make a file for the timeout watcher"
  latefile=$(mktemp "$work/late.XXXXXX") || hold "cannot make a file for the timeout watcher"
  copy=
  if [ "$WHAT" = "the base's copy of $TEST" ]; then
    copy=$(mktemp "$work/copy.XXXXXX") || hold "cannot make a file for the copy of $TEST"
    git -C "$REPO" show "$basesha:$TEST" >"$copy" || hold "cannot read $TEST from $bbranch at $bat"
  fi
  t0=$(date +%s)
  (
    cd "$WT" || exit 2
    # The landing run's switch is set here, never inherited: a depot's command
    # reads an empty one as no landing, and this run is the pull request's.
    export YARDR_LANDING=1
    # The tests of a pull request have no use for the App's id and key file,
    # which are for the token alone.
    unset GITHUB_APP_ID GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE
    # The test command starts with no descriptor above 2 that the run did
    # not give it, or does not start, as merge-local's does for a landing.
    closefds "the tests"
    if [ -n "$copy" ]; then exec sh "$copy"; else exec sh -c "$TEST"; fi
  ) >"$log" 2>&1 </dev/null &
  pid=$!
  (
    nap=
    trap 'kill "$nap" 2>/dev/null; exit 0' TERM
    # The sleep holds none of the run's inherited files. What this sh cannot
    # close stays open here, and the test command's start above refuses it,
    # so a watcher left holding one belongs to a run that is already over.
    closefds
    # The deadline counts only time not spent waiting for the race lock: a
    # run late in the queue would otherwise be killed with every test
    # green. Sleep $LIMIT, then sleep again for the waited seconds not yet
    # credited, repeating until no new wait is found; only then mark late
    # and kill. The gate prints a start marker while it waits and a final
    # `test: waited N s` line when it acquires the lock; account for the
    # elapsed active wait too, since it may outlast LIMIT. The loop is
    # merge-local's, copied: the scripts are installed one file each, and
    # merge-local also ships in the packs, so there is no shared file for
    # them to source. A fix to one is made in all (a test holds them equal).
    credited=0
    parred=0
    wait_for=$LIMIT
    while :; do
      sleep "$wait_for" &
      nap=$!
      echo "$nap" >"$napfile"
      wait "$nap"
      waited=$(awk -v now="$(date +%s)" '
        /^test: race lock wait started at [0-9]+$/ { active=$NF; next }
        /^test: waited [0-9]+ s for the race lock$/ { total+=$3; active="" }
        END { if (active != "") total += now-active; print total+0 }
      ' "$log" 2>/dev/null)
      case "$waited" in ''|*[!0-9]*) waited=0 ;; esac
      # A race leg the gate throttled with -p runs slower by design, so the
      # deadline follows it: cores over -p, floored at 1, times LIMIT. The
      # gate says the throttle in one line; without it the factor is 1 and
      # nothing changes. Normalize the decimal timeout before shell
      # arithmetic, which otherwise treats a value such as 08 as octal.
      # A nonnumeric LIMIT holds the factor at 1 so the fuse remains active.
      throttle=$(awk '
        /^test: go test -p=[0-9]+ [(]load [0-9.]+ on [0-9]+ cores[)]$/ {
          p = $4; sub(/^-p=/, "", p); c = $8
          if (p > 0 && c > 0) { f = int(c / p); if (f < 1) f = 1; fact = f; par = p; cores = c }
        }
        END { if (fact != "") print fact":"par":"cores }
      ' "$log" 2>/dev/null)
      fact=${throttle%%:*}
      parcores=${throttle#*:}
      par=${parcores%%:*}
      cores=${parcores#*:}
      case "$fact" in ''|*[!0-9]*) fact=1 ;; esac
      case "$LIMIT" in
        ''|*[!0-9]*) fact=1; limitnum=0 ;;
        *)
          limitnum=$LIMIT
          while [ "${limitnum#0}" != "$limitnum" ]; do limitnum=${limitnum#0}; done
          [ -n "$limitnum" ] || limitnum=0
          ;;
      esac
      extension=0
      [ "$fact" -gt 1 ] && extension=$(((fact - 1) * limitnum))
      extra=$((waited - credited + extension - parred))
      if [ "$extra" -le 0 ]; then
        break
      fi
      # Past the break the deadline grew: say so once, in the run's log.
      if [ "$parred" -eq 0 ] && [ "$fact" -gt 1 ]; then
        echo "deadline $LIMIT s x $fact for -p=$par on $cores cores" >>"$log"
      fi
      credited=$waited
      parred=$extension
      wait_for=$extra
    done
    echo late >"$latefile"
    seen=
    while t=$(tree "$pid") && [ "$t" != "$seen" ]; do
      # shellcheck disable=SC2086
      kill -STOP $t 2>/dev/null
      seen=$t
    done
    # Whatever ps said, the command itself goes.
    # shellcheck disable=SC2086
    kill -KILL ${seen:-$pid} 2>/dev/null
  ) &
  watch=$!
  # The watcher records its sleep before it waits on it; hold until it has
  # (a bounded wait), so the traps never kill a watcher whose sleep is
  # not yet known.
  i=0
  while [ ! -s "$napfile" ] && [ "$i" -lt 100 ]; do sleep 0.05; i=$((i + 1)); done
  wait "$pid" && rc=0 || rc=$?
  pid=
  secs=$((secs + $(date +%s) - t0))
  unwatched
  # A command that passed as its deadline came has passed. The marker is
  # empty until the watcher writes to it, so the deadline came if it holds
  # something.
  late=
  if [ -s "$latefile" ] && [ "$rc" != 0 ]; then
    late=1
    echo "pr-ci: the tests ran past their deadline of ${LIMIT}s and were killed" >>"$log"
  fi
  lines=$(wc -l <"$log" | tr -d ' ')
  out=$(tail -n "$TAIL" "$log")
  # The failing tests a red note keeps ahead of the tail: the full log's
  # FAIL lines (go test's, a shell gate's FAIL:, XCTest's), and the Gradle
  # names from the checkout, deduped and capped, so a failure above the tail
  # still shows. Only when the log runs past the tail; a short run's tail
  # already holds the log's names.
  fails=
  if [ "$lines" -gt "$TAIL" ]; then
    fails=$( { grep -E -e '--- FAIL' -e '^FAIL' -e "Test Case '-\[.*\]' failed" "$log" 2>/dev/null; gradle_fails; } | awk '!seen[$0]++' | head -n "$FAILS" || true)
  fi
  if [ "$rc" = 0 ]; then
    rundone
    log=
  else
    # A red run's full log stays standing until the outcome attaches it to
    # the bead (attach_log); the run's other files go as ever.
    kept=$log
    log=
    rundone
    log=$kept
  fi
  latefile=
  napfile=
  copy=
  printf '%s\n' "$out"
}
# What a note says of a run's output: all of it, or its end.
shown() {
  if [ "$lines" -gt "$TAIL" ]; then echo "the last $TAIL of $lines lines of output"; else echo "its output"; fi
}
# attach_log carries a red run's full log to the bead, so the names and the
# tail in the outcome's note stay readable after the checkout and the run's
# files are gone. It runs before the outcome moves the bead (bye takes the
# log with it), and never fails the outcome: a refused attach leaves a note
# saying the output was too large to keep instead.
attach_log() {
  [ -n "${log:-}" ] && [ -f "$log" ] || return 0
  "$Y" bead note "$BEAD" "pr-ci: full gate output of the failed run on $url at $tested ($WHAT, exit $rc)" --file "$log" >/dev/null 2>&1 && return 0
  "$Y" bead note "$BEAD" "pr-ci: the full gate output of the failed run on $url at $tested ($WHAT, exit $rc) was too large to keep" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
  return 0
}

# The yard keeps its disk before the tests run, as it does for a landing (yardr
# disk keep): under disk_keep_gb free it rotates GOCACHE and clears stale
# go-tmp, so the build has room, and a full disk is not read as a red run of the
# pull request. Best effort: a keep that fails is in the log, and the run goes
# on to the tests, which fail loudly themselves when the disk is what ails them.
# Where the installed yardr predates the command the step is skipped. Cobra may
# return success and print the root help for an unknown subcommand with --help,
# so require keep's own usage.
if "$Y" disk keep --help 2>&1 | grep -q '^  yardr disk keep \[flags\]$'; then
  keepout=$("$Y" disk keep 2>&1) && ok=1 || ok=
  if [ -n "$keepout" ]; then
    printf '%s\n' "$keepout"
  fi
  if [ -z "$ok" ]; then
    echo "== $BEAD disk keep failed, running the tests on"
  fi
fi

secs=0
runtest
if [ "$rc" -gt 128 ] && [ -z "$late" ]; then
  first="the tests on $url at $tested ($WHAT) were ended by signal $((rc - 128)) (exit $rc), not by a failing test"
  echo "== $first; running them once more"
  "$Y" bead note "$BEAD" "pr-ci: $first; running them once more on the same commit. Of the first run, $(shown):
${fails:+$fails
}$out" || echo "== could not note the first run on $BEAD"
  out1=$out fails1=$fails
  # The first run's log is noted; only the second run's decides the
  # outcome, so only it is attached. Gone before the second run starts, it
  # cannot leak whatever the second does.
  rm -f "$log"
  log=
  runtest
  [ "$rc" -le 128 ] || [ -n "$late" ] || { attach_log; hold "$first, and again by signal $((rc - 128)) (exit $rc) when run once more. Nothing was decided about the pull request: find what sends the signal, then unhold to test it again.
First run:
${fails1:+$fails1
}$out1
Second run:
${fails:+$fails
}$out"; }
fi
if [ -n "$late" ]; then
  attach_log
  report changes "the tests on $url at $tested ($WHAT) ran past their deadline of ${LIMIT}s and were killed; $(shown):
${fails:+$fails
}$out"
fi
case $rc in
0) ;;
2) attach_log; hold "the tests could not run on $url at $tested ($WHAT said so: exit 2); $(shown):
${fails:+$fails
}$out" ;;
*) attach_log; report changes "the tests failed on $url at $tested ($WHAT, exit $rc); $(shown):
${fails:+$fails
}$out" ;;
esac

# Green. It counts for the head the tests ran on and no other: a pull request
# pushed to during the run has a head they said nothing of. GitHub is asked once
# more, and only the same head is recorded; the base is not asked again, as
# GitHub asks nothing again of a pull request between its checks and its merge.
now=$(live_head)
[ -n "$now" ] ||
  hold "the tests passed on $url at $tested ($WHAT), but GitHub would not say what its head is now: $(head -c 500 "$errf"). Nothing was recorded; unhold to run them again."
[ "$(lower "$now")" = "$(lower "$head")" ] ||
  report changes "the PR changed while CI ran: the tests passed on $tested ($WHAT), but the head is $(short "$now") now. Nothing was recorded; the next commits bring it back to review."
"$Y" bead update "$BEAD" --meta "ci_head=$head" >"$errf" 2>&1 ||
  hold "the tests passed on $url at $tested ($WHAT), but ci_head could not be recorded on $BEAD: $(head -c 500 "$errf"). Unhold to run them again."
# The gate's own last line, when that is what the run ended with, is the record
# of what it ran; nothing else of the output is copied on a green run.
last=$(printf '%s\n' "$out" | tail -n 1)
case $last in "test: "*) said=" Its last line: $last" ;; *) said= ;; esac
report "done" "the tests passed on $url at $head merged into $bbranch at $basesha ($WHAT, $secs s). CI is the yard's own run of the depot's test command on that merge: ci_head=$head.$said"
